Student Academic Survival & Dropout Prediction

Overview

This project was developed as the Module 6 Final Data Science
Project. It analyzes longitudinal student academic data to identify
patterns associated with the Target_Dropout_Next_Sem variable and
builds machine learning models for classification.

The project covers the complete data science workflow, including data
cleaning, exploratory data analysis (EDA), data visualization, feature
preparation, categorical encoding, leakage-aware train-test splitting,
machine learning, model evaluation, model comparison, and
feature-importance analysis.

Project Objective

The objective is to:

Analyze academic, financial, workload, and engagement-related
student features.

Identify patterns associated with the dropout target.

Build classification models to predict Target_Dropout_Next_Sem.

Compare Logistic Regression and Random Forest performance.

Identify features that contribute most strongly to Random Forest
predictions.

Dataset

The dataset contains:

79,239 semester-level records

20,000 unique students

Multiple semester records for individual students

An imbalanced target variable:

Class 0: 72,322 records (91.27%)

Class 1: 6,917 records (8.73%)

Because the dataset is longitudinal, Student_ID was used as a grouping
variable during the train-test split so that records from the same
student did not appear in both sets.

Dataset source: Add the original public/Kaggle dataset link here
before publishing the repository.

Project Workflow

Dataset loading and inspection

Missing-value and duplicate checks

Median imputation for Family_Income and LMS_Logins

Target-variable analysis

Exploratory data analysis and visualization

Feature and target selection

Cleaning inconsistent categorical labels

One-hot encoding of Gender and Housing_Status

Group-based train-test split using Student_ID

Logistic Regression training and evaluation

Random Forest training and evaluation

Model comparison

Random Forest feature-importance analysis

Results summary and conclusion

Exploratory Data Analysis

Several variables showed associations with the target.

Some of the notable observations were:

Average Sem_GPA was lower for class 1 than class 0.

Average Financial_Stress was higher for class 1.

Average Failed_Courses was higher for class 1.

Average Work_Hours was higher for class 1.

Average Attendance and LMS_Logins were lower for class 1.

First-generation students had a higher proportion of class-1 records
in this dataset.

Students with scholarships had a lower proportion of class-1 records
in this dataset.

These are associations in the analyzed data and should not be
interpreted as causal relationships.

Machine Learning Models

Two classification models were evaluated:

Logistic Regression

Random Forest Classifier

Because the target is highly imbalanced, balanced class weights were
used. Performance was evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall,
F1-score, and confusion matrices rather than accuracy alone.

Model Results

Model              Accuracy      Precision  Recall (Class       F1-Score
(Class 1)             1)      (Class 1)

Logistic              72.8%          0.194          0.647          0.299
Regression

Interpretation

Logistic Regression detected a larger proportion of actual class-1
cases, with 64.7% class-1 recall, but produced more false-positive
predictions.

Random Forest achieved higher overall accuracy and higher class-1
precision, but its 24.2% class-1 recall means that many actual
class-1 cases were missed.

This demonstrates why accuracy alone is not sufficient for evaluating an
imbalanced classification problem.

Important Predictive Features

Random Forest feature importance showed that the model relied most
strongly on:

Sem_GPA

Financial_Stress

Work_Hours

Attendance

Family_Income

LMS_Logins

Failed_Courses

Feature importance represents predictive contribution within the Random
Forest model and does not establish causation.

